 # codex-app-server
 
+## Manager record catalog experiment
+
+The patched runtime accepts `CODEX_MANAGER_RECORD_CATALOG`, an absolute path to
+a manager-owned JSON manifest. This explicitly makes the instance a **read-only
+record viewer**. Native history endpoints use the manifest. `thread/resume` is a
+read facade for registered projections; it does not activate an agent. Execution,
+mutation, injected history, and resume-by-path are rejected before dispatch.
+
+Alternatively, `CODEX_MANAGER_SHARED_CATALOG` uses the same manifest in a normal
+working instance. `thread/list` merges its native records with foreign projections,
+retaining native IDs and omitting duplicate projections of that instance's home.
+Normal native requests retain the existing account, permission, ownership and
+writer checks. Projection reads use the read-only adapter and projection mutations
+are rejected. The two catalog environment variables are mutually exclusive.
+Neither catalog mode transfers execution ownership to the authenticated account.
+
+Manifest version 1 is `{ "version": 1, "revision": "stable-content-revision",
+"hostId": "local", "entries": [...] }`. Each entry has `projectionThreadId`
+(a distinct UUID), `threadId` (the source UUID), `hostId`, `sourceStoreId`,
+`codexHome`, and `rolloutPath`. Paths must be absolute on this runtime's host;
+the verified rollout must remain under that home's `sessions` or
+`archived_sessions` directory and its metadata ID must match. Projection IDs
+must not collide with any registered native ID. All entries must belong to the
+manifest's single host. The manager retains the canonical host/store/ID mapping.
+
+Legacy JSONL records and authoritative paginated state/history databases are read
+without copying, migrating, repairing, or acquiring a writer for a foreign store.
+The canonical host/store/ID scope also applies to projected collaboration links.
+Fresh reads observe source changes; this alone does not refresh an already open
+renderer. The manager refreshes membership metadata in the background.
+
+Shared listing uses a query-bound cursor and retains at most 4,096 native records
+plus 4,096 manifest entries per request. Native metadata changes invalidate its
+cursor. Project and section filters use stored metadata in shared mode; project
+registries themselves are not federated. Ancestry queries and section-position
+sorting still require a common ordering adapter. Live renderer notifications and
+native UI acceptance are separate integration work. Removing the environment
+variable before launching a new instance restores ordinary routing.
+
 `codex app-server` is the interface Codex uses to power rich interfaces such as the [Codex VS Code extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=openai.chatgpt).
 
