+### Managed common record observation (experimental)
+
+`CODEX_MANAGER_SHARED_CATALOG` enables a manager-generated local catalog alongside
+the current profile's native records. `CODEX_MANAGER_RECORD_CATALOG` instead enables
+a dedicated reader. The two modes are mutually exclusive. Foreign records use
+namespaced projection IDs, `path: null`, and `canAcceptDirectInput: false`.
+Their `thread/resume` is a read-only subscription; it does not load an execution
+actor, change ownership, or copy authentication or history into the reader's HOME.
+Native records in shared mode keep their usual execution behavior.
+Input, steering, interruption, injected items and realtime operations targeting
+an already loaded native actor do not wait for shared catalog discovery. They
+still pass through normal execution and writer gates. This isolation requires
+all thread references in the request to identify that actor; it does not apply
+to dedicated readers, cold record resume, or foreign projections.
+
+Managed SSH runtimes may use a version 2 source manifest instead of the version 1
+explicit record manifest. It contains `version: 2`, `hostId: "local"`, and `sources`
+with `hostId`, `sourceStoreId` (`manager:<profile UUID>`), and absolute `codexHome`.
+Every home must have a matching `managed-source.json`. The native runtime discovers
+active and archived rows through existing read-only state databases using ID-keyset
+pages. This inventory includes V2 children with empty previews so linked history
+remains readable even when ordinary sidebar filters hide those records. Discovery
+has a two-second cache and an eight-second timeout. It neither initializes a
+foreign database nor reads foreign configuration or credentials. Discovery is
+limited to 256 sources and a 4-MiB source descriptor; invalid sources produce a
+catalog error. The older version 1 explicit record manifest remains limited to
+4,096 records and 4 MiB. Source inventories read indexed metadata in batches of
+256, including empty-preview and archived records, without the explicit-manifest
+record cap. A newly enrolled empty source can appear before its first native database.
+Version 3 adds `legacySources`, with the same home fields but a source ID of
+`legacy:<SHA-256 of the UTF-8 codexHome string>`. These homes require no managed
+marker or writer grant. Indexed stores use their existing read-only state pages;
+unindexed stores discover session headers under their own `sessions` and
+`archived_sessions` directories. Header discovery reads at most eight lines and
+1 MiB per `.jsonl` file, visits at most 32,768 paths per source, and never creates
+a state index. Unindexed compressed files are not included yet. Duplicate legacy
+thread paths without an authoritative database, malformed headers, unindexed
+paginated histories and unavailable sources produce a catalog error. The shared
+256-source, 4-MiB descriptor and timeout limits still apply; the unindexed path
+scan is still bounded. Session directory symlinks are enumerated under
+their separately registered canonical home. A record whose index exists only in
+another registered home's DB is still assigned to the physical owner of its
+rollout. Directory aliases are resolved once per inventory, not by opening every
+history file. Duplicate rows produce one projection: the owning store's metadata
+wins when present, otherwise the newest indexed copy supplies display metadata.
+An indexed copy cannot enroll an unregistered destination. When a profile shares
+session files but keeps its databases separately, the chosen registered index
+supplies read-only metadata. Source databases remain confined to that index's home;
+rollouts and lineage ancestors must resolve inside explicitly registered session
+roots. Opening paginated history reconstructs an unnamed private SQLite history
+index with the native projector and schema. It incrementally processes complete
+durable lines, including reference lineage, without creating or repairing any
+source history database or acquiring a recorder. Immutable IDs and durable
+checkpoint coverage are still verified; ambiguous ancestors and incomplete
+projections fail explicitly. The private index is shared across discovery refreshes
+within the same source scope and is removed by SQLite when its connection closes.
+Source metadata connections are reopened independently of that private cache.
+Changes to the effective registered roots, including a referenced inventory update,
+discard retained private indexes; readers already using them keep their own handles.
+Legacy records without any indexed row are read from the validated canonical file;
+paginated records still require a registered metadata row and valid durable rollout.
+The general-purpose read-only store remains strict unless private projection is
+explicitly enabled by its caller.
+
+Indexed inventories retain metadata summaries in the short-lived discovery
+snapshot. Listing and new-record notifications do not open every rollout file;
+opening a selected record still validates its current source and history. A
+missing history file can therefore remain visible in the source index and fail
+only when opened. Discovery currently materializes all metadata in memory and
+refreshes its pages after the cache expires; this is not a persistent incremental
+index. Live inventory cursors bind to the registered source scope and query, so
+a newer record does not invalidate a cursor for older records. Newer records are
+observed through notifications or a fresh first page; the cursor is not a frozen
+cross-source snapshot. Changing the registered source scope invalidates it.
+If a child record outlives its registered parent, the view clears unresolved
+parent/fork links and retains a generic subagent origin plus its nickname/role.
+It never exposes the unprojected parent ID as a native navigation target. The
+source history is unchanged, and other records remain listable.
+
+A version 3 descriptor may leave `sources` empty and set `managedSourcesPath` to
+an absolute version 2 source inventory in a separate file. Discovery rereads this
+inventory after the short cache expires; recursive references are rejected. SSH
+helpers publish `catalog-sources.json` for older runtimes and a separate
+`catalog-mixed-sources.json` for runtimes with the mixed-source capability. An
+older profile's enrollment can update the managed inventory without replacing
+the legacy source descriptor. Existing legacy source enrollment is discovered
+from `~/.codex` and llm-usage metadata when a capable managed profile starts;
+records created later in an enrolled home are discovered natively without
+republishing the descriptor. A newly registered legacy home requires another
+enrollment publication. These manifests are read-only view configuration and
+never add legacy homes to the managed execution allowlist.
+
+Read-only projections expose experimental `thread.extra.managedRecord` containing
+`hostId`, `sourceStoreId`, and `canonicalThreadId`. List entries, membership pushes,
+and read/resume responses carry the same origin. Canonical execution threads do
+not set this origin. Transport activity observers can therefore distinguish
+another profile's visible work from work running in the current profile without
+making another catalog request. Unscoped startup/configuration requests in shared
+mode do not wait for catalog discovery; record operations still validate it.
+
+After the resume response, a connection-scoped watcher polls durable history about
+once per second. New turns and changed items are sent using the existing
+`turn/started`, `item/started`, `item/completed`, and `turn/completed` notifications.
+Partial persisted items are upserts; unflushed model deltas are not replicated.
+The reader preserves the persisted turn ID and uses projected thread references.
+It never forwards tool or approval requests belonging to the source execution.
+Identical snapshots do not generate duplicate completion events.
+
+`thread/unsubscribe` cancels only that connection's subscription and returns
+`unsubscribed`; disconnect also cancels its subscriptions. Other readers and the
+canonical writer continue independently. There are at most 64 subscriptions per
+runtime, with catch-up bounded to 32 turns, 2,048 items, 64 item pages, and 8 MiB of
+serialized items. Missing/replaced history, a removed source, exceeded bounds or a
+10-second read timeout retains the last valid snapshot and emits one
+`thread/status/changed` with `systemError`. The watcher retries while subscribed;
+if a valid continuation becomes readable, it clears the error and sends the
+persisted changes without another resume request. Identity, history continuity,
+and size checks still apply to every retry. Replaced history or an exceeded
+catch-up window can require reopening the record; the watcher does not silently
+rebuild the view from a different history.
+
+A successful `thread/list` also subscribes that connection to catalog membership.
+After its response, a separate watcher checks the manifest about every two seconds.
+Added readable projections emit native `thread/started` notifications with
+`canAcceptDirectInput: false`; they do not load execution actors. Removed projections
+emit `thread/deleted` only for their projection IDs, removing the client's cached
+view without deleting canonical records. Changed rollout pointers can emit
+`thread/archived` and `thread/unarchived`. Shared mode omits projections of its own
+HOME because those records already use native notifications.
+
+Repeated list requests reuse the connection's watcher. Disconnect cancels it;
+unsubscribing from one record does not unsubscribe from the list. At most 64
+connections are observed. Metadata reads use concurrency 16 and rotating batches
+of at most 64 changes, with a 10-second batch timeout. Invalid/unavailable manifests
+retain the last valid membership until a valid manifest returns. Individual
+unreadable additions are retried without preventing later additions from appearing.
+Names from delivered list pages and newly pushed records are also observed.
+Each poll checks a rotating batch of up to 256 known names through the canonical
+metadata reader and emits `thread/name/updated` only when the name changes,
+including clearing a name. Later list pages join the existing subscription.
+The name check needs neither `thread/resume` nor a manifest change, and it does
+not replace or hydrate the client's cached history. Failed metadata reads retain
+the previous name and are retried on a later pass. Unflushed token deltas are not
+replicated. The manager's serving backend refreshes the manifest independently of
+UI requests.
+Client rendering and project grouping must be verified separately from delivery
+of these notifications.
+
+In a managed execution profile, `thread/name/set` uses the loaded actor's existing
+canonical writer. The request participates in the same per-thread admission gate
+as turns and cannot write after that instance has released the record. A cold or
+list-only reference must first be resumed in its assigned profile; a rename never
+loads an actor, changes ownership, or starts a model turn. Catalog projections
+remain read-only. Empty-name notifications can be delivered by the server, but
+renderers may ignore them; the normal name setter requires a nonempty name.
+
+### Managed profile handoff: close an idle subtree (experimental)
+
+`thread/managedIdleStatus` observes a loaded managed root and its known spawn
+descendants without pausing dispatch or changing actor admission. Its response
+includes `hostId`, `sourceStoreId`, `observedThreadIds`, an `idle` boolean, and
+bounded `blockers` entries containing `threadId` and `kind`. `proofScope` is
+always `"advisory"`: a later request can create work immediately after the
+observation. Update coordinators can inspect this endpoint before entering
+maintenance, but must still complete the strict close below before transferring
+ownership or replacing a runtime.
+
+An unloaded descendant reports `coldDescendantRequiresWriterClaim` and keeps
+`idle` false. A coordinator may proceed to strict close when this is the only
+blocker category: strict close must acquire the actual canonical writer claim
+and validate its persisted work before returning any release proof.
+
+`thread/managedCloseIdle` is available only to a managed instance whose loaded
+root actor has an immutable, allowlisted canonical source binding. Enable
+experimental API capability on initialization. It is separate from unsubscribe:
+it closes the root and its loaded spawn descendants without stopping the server
+or unrelated threads.
+
+```json
+{ "method": "thread/managedCloseIdle", "id": 21, "params": { "threadId": "01900000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001" } }
+{ "id": 21, "result": { "threadId": "01900000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001", "closedThreadIds": ["01900000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001"], "writerReleaseVerified": true } }
+```
+
+The server excludes new mutations for the exact subtree, pauses durable queue
+dispatch, and refuses active turns, agent mail, queued submissions, approvals,
+background terminals, pending hooks, and unknown descendant state. It then closes
+descendants before parents and requires successful final flushes, writer shutdowns, actor
+termination, and listener drain before returning the proof. Bound storage also
+fences callbacks after release. A `turn/completed` notification alone is not an
+idle proof; it can precede final runtime cleanup, so a close may briefly refuse.
+
+The operation is bounded to 128 records and 60 seconds. For an unloaded known
+descendant it retains the canonical writer lock, validates persisted state, and
+closes only the recorder. It does not start a model, hooks, or MCP services. A
+competing writer or unknown state causes refusal. All records must belong to the same managed source.
+The original instance remains fenced for successfully closed records; assignment
+back to it needs a new verified ownership activation, not an automatic resume.
+
+Errors, disconnects, and timeouts do not authorize another owner to write. A busy
+refusal before any actor starts closing leaves admission open. Once any actor
+may have closed, a partial failure retains the source fence for explicit recovery.
+No turn or queued input is automatically replayed by this API.
+
+### Managed instance maintenance
+
+`server/managedShutdown` is experimental and available only in a managed standalone
+server. Send `{ "processId": 1234 }` using the process ID from `server/diagnostics`.
+It rejects a different process ID or an embedder without a standalone lifecycle hook.
+
+The server reserves admission across all connections, including requests queued or
+still running after disconnect. Active standalone commands, login/setup operations,
+client callbacks and non-idle root/child actors prevent shutdown. It observes all
+loaded roots before attempting strict writer close, bounds the inventory to 1024
+records and the close phase to 60 seconds, and never interrupts a turn. Unknown or
+unbound actors prevent shutdown. This operation is for profile settings and updates;
+shared conversation lists do not invoke it.
+
+Success returns `processId`, `closedThreadIds`, `shutdownRequested: true` and
+`writerReleaseVerified: true`. The transport writer acknowledges this response
+before the server requests graceful exit. The caller must independently verify
+the original process exited and its profile lock was released before restarting.
+An error, lost response or timeout is not exit evidence. A failed attempt restores
+global admission and same-owner admission for fully verified closes; any uncertain
+partial subtree close retains its existing fence. No input is replayed.
+
